+  \]
+  
+  These force (a_{10}=a_{11}=0), then (a_9=0), contradicting the
+  branch.
+
+Thus there is no actual (C\ne0) point on which extra digit constancy could
+be read.  The nonconstant-digit and degree statements above are properties
+of Moh's necessary *shape*, not consequences sampled from a solution.
+
+Source caution: p.210 visibly prints that two remainders have (y)-degree
+less than four, while its next displayed equation with a nonzero
+(f^{4/10}) term forces one to have degree four.  The frozen m2 report uses
+the coherent (<5) repair.  This report does not use that disputed bound in
+the Jacobian certificate.
+
+### 4.2 The ((16,12;M_2=13,V_2=3,k=1)) shape
+
+**SOURCE-READ, pp.207--209.**  Put
+
+\[
+h=y^3(y-x)+b_1y^3+b_2y^2+b_3y+b_4=yA+b_4
+=y^2B+b_3y+b_4.
+\]
+
+Moh's p.208 pair is
+
+\[
+\bar g=h^4+\alpha_1h^3+\alpha_2h^2+\alpha_3h+\alpha_4,qquad
+\bar f=h^3+\beta_2h+\beta_3,
+\]
+
+with
+
+\[
+\begin{array}{ll}
+\alpha_1\in k,&\\
+\alpha_2=c_1A+c_2,&\beta_2=c_3A+c_4,\\
+\alpha_3=c_5A+c_5B+c_7,&\beta_3=c_8A+c_9B+c_{10},\\
+\alpha_4=c_{11}A+c_{12}B+c_{13}(y-x).&
+\end{array}
+\]
+
+The repeated (c_5) is literal in the page image and (c_6) is absent.
+Taking the print literally gives
+
+\[
+4\ (b_i)+1\ (\alpha_1)+12\ (\text{distinct displayed }c_i)=17.
+\]
+
+There is also a coherent editorial/gauge reading: replace the second (c_5)
+by an independent (c_6) and do not count the removable constant
+(\alpha_1); this again gives (4+13=17).  These are different families,
+so the driver keeps them separate.  The literal general family yields 77
+coefficient equations for (J(\bar f,\bar g)=Cx); it is generated and sized,
+not claimed solved.  Its five nonconstant outer digits all have
+(x)-degree one, while (\alpha_1) is the one explicitly constant digit.
+
+Moh next uses the (\eta)-series constants and the target change
+
+\[
+(f,g)\longmapsto(f-3a_3/4,\ g-a_1f-a_4).
+\]
+
+Writing
+
+\[
+\beta_2\beta_3=\Gamma h+\Gamma^*,qquad
+\beta_2^3=\Delta h^2+\Delta^*,
+\]
+
+the normalized pair is
+
+\[
+\begin{aligned}
+f={}&h^3+\beta_2h+\beta_3,\\
+g={}&h^4+\frac43(\beta_2h^2+\beta_3h)
+ +\frac29(\beta_2^2+2\Gamma)-\frac4{81}\Delta+a_2h^2,
+\end{aligned}
+\]
+
+up to a removable scalar constant.  Here
+
+\[
+\beta_2=c_3A+c_4,qquad \beta_3=c_8A+c_9B+c_{10}.
+\]
+
+This gives the exact positive control
+
+\[
+4\ (h)+2\ (\beta_2)+3\ (\beta_3)+1\ (a_2)=10.
+\]
+
+The driver performs Euclidean (h)-division and checks all eight embedding
+relations.  In the independent-(c_6), gauged-(\alpha_1) reading they are
+
+\[
+\begin{gathered}
+c_1=4c_3/3,quad c_2=a_2+4c_4/3,quad
+c_5=4c_8/3,quad c_6=2c_3^2/9+4c_9/3,\\
+c_7=4c_{10}/3,\\
+c_{11}=2b_3c_3^2/9+4c_3c_4/9,\\
+c_{12}=-2b_4c_3^2/9+4c_3c_8/9,quad
+c_{13}=-4c_3^3/81+4c_3c_9/9.
+\end{gathered}
+\]
+
+There are five nonconstant canonical digits, two in (f) and three in
+(g); after exact reduction every one has (x)-degree at most one, hence at
+most (k+1=2).
+
+**PROVED-HERE / exact coefficient certificate.**  The normalized equation
+(J(f,g)=Cx) gives 36 coefficients.  On (C\ne0), its (x^1y^0)
+coefficient gives
+
+\[
+C=\frac4{81}c_3c_9(c_3^2-9c_9),
+\]
+
+so all three factors are nonzero.  Further coefficients force
+(a_2=0, c_8=-b_4c_3).  With (r=c_9/c_3^2), they then give
+
+\[
+E(r)=54r^2-36r+5=0.
+\]
+
+Exact gcd computations against (5-18r), (1-3r), (6r-1), and
+(4r-1) successively force (b_4=0), solve (c_4,c_{10}), and force
+(b_3=b_2=0).  The final coefficient forces (9r-2=0), but
+(\gcd(E,9r-2)=1).  Thus the normalized (C\ne0) locus is empty.
+
+Again, `SATURATED-EMPTY` is a statement about the normalized family only.
+The literal 17-parameter print was not silently identified with the
+independent-(c_6) family.
+
+### 4.3 What the two positive controls actually measure
+
+| row | source stage | scalar parameters | nonconstant outer digits | maximum (x)-degree | (C\ne0) result here |
+|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| ((15,10),k=2) | pp.210--211, after second simplification | 12, not 22 | 3 | 2 | empty |
+| ((16,12),k=1) | p.208 literal | 17 | 5 (plus constant (\alpha_1)) | 1 | general system not solved |
+| ((16,12),k=1) | p.209 normalized | 10 | 5 dependent digits | 1 | empty |
+
+The table is the discriminator's main lesson.  Moh's scalar counts and the
+number of (h)-adic indices are different quantities.  Moreover, an empty
+Jacobian locus cannot support a nonvacuous inference about what coefficients
+of an existing pair are forced to be constant.
+
+## 5. Uniform negative control
+
+### 5.1 Construction
+
+**PROVED-HERE / UNREVIEWED.**  Fix
+
+\[
+k\ge0,qquad p,r\ge2,qquad e>k+1,
+\]
+
+and an arbitrary monic polynomial
+
+\[
+\phi(T)=T^e+a_{e-1}T^{e-1}+\cdots+a_0.
+\]
+
+Set
+
+\[
+X=\gamma+\pi,qquad
+Y=\frac{\gamma^{k+1}}{k+1},qquad
+U=Y+\phi(X),qquad
+H=X+U^r,
+\]
+
+and
+
+\[
+P=H^p+U,qquad Q=H.
+\]
+
+In the independent coordinates (X,Y),
+(J_{X,Y}(P,Q)=-1), while
+(J_{\gamma,\pi}(X,Y)=-\gamma^k).  Therefore
+
+\[
+J_{\gamma,\pi}(P,Q)=\gamma^k.
+\]
+
+Let (d=re).  Since (e>k+1) and (\phi) is monic,
+
+\[
+\deg H=\deg_\pi H=d,qquad
+\deg P=\deg_\pi P=pd,qquad
+\deg Q=\deg_\pi Q=d,
+\]
+
+and all three relevant leaders are monic in (\pi).  The canonical expansion
+has reduced pair ((p,q)=(p,1)):
+
+\[
+P=H^p+A_0,quad A_0=U,qquad Q=H,qquad
+\deg_\pi U=e<d.
+\]
+
+There is exactly one nonconstant outer digit, but
+
+\[
+\deg_\gamma A_0=e=d/r>k+1.
+\]
+
+This disproves the global degree clause for every (k), not only for Keller
+pairs.
+
+### 5.2 Keller case and growing data
+
+At (k=0), (Y=\gamma), and every step is an elementary polynomial
+automorphism.  An explicit inverse is
+
+\[
+U=P-Q^p,qquad X=Q-U^r,qquad
+\gamma=U-\phi(X),qquad \pi=X-\gamma.
+\]
+
+Thus this is a Keller automorphism in Moh's monic total-degree (=)
+(\pi)-degree gauge for every (e).  It is a strengthened version of the
+two-shear ((y+x^2,x+(y+x^2)^3)) control: for the basic two-shear pair the
+growing data can be hidden inside the chosen approximate root; the added
+outer shear exposes it as (A_0=U).
+
+The exact ladder output depends on what “coefficient” means:
+
+- **Outer digits:** one nonconstant digit, independent of (d).  This part
+  does not fail; it is the degree claim that fails.
+
+- **Inner (\pi)-coefficient functions:** expanding
+  
+  \[
+  U=\frac{\gamma^{k+1}}{k+1}+\phi(\gamma+\pi)
+   =\sum_{b=0}^{e}u_b(\gamma)\pi^b,
+  \]
+  
+  every (u_b) for (b<e) has nonzero leading term
+  (inom eb\gamma^{e-b}).  Hence (e=\Theta(d)) inner coefficients are
+  nonconstant, their largest (\gamma)-degree is (e), and
+  (a_0,\ldots,a_{e-1}) give (e) free scalar parameters.
+
+This is the requested exact negative-control statement.  “Automorphisms
+exist in every degree” alone would not refute a literal outer-index bound;
+the displayed family identifies the unbounded degree and inner-data growth.
+
+### 5.3 Boundary of the disproof
+
+The counterfamily has (q=1).  It does **not** decide
+`OPEN[T-LADDER-q>=2]`, in particular the ((3,2)) and ((4,3)) descended
+strata.  Polynomial automorphisms naturally occupy this divisibility
+boundary, so no conclusion for (q\ge2) is drawn by analogy.
+
+There is nevertheless a second obstruction to the advertised “finite
+computation.”  Even if only (B=B(k,p,q)) outer digits are nonconstant and
+each satisfies (\deg_\gamma\le k+1), the canonical condition only says
+(\deg_\pi<d).  One such digit can contain up to
+((k+2)d) scalar monomials.  A bounded number of such digits is still
+(\Theta(d)) input, unless a separate theorem bounds or determines their
+(\pi)-profiles.
+
+## 6. Reading: consequences for theorem (T)
+
+### 6.1 No degree-independent finite computation follows
+
+The answer to the unrestricted reading question is **no**:
+
+- on the outer-index reading the count is trivially bounded by (p+q), but
+  the (\gamma)-degree clause is false;
+- on the scalar/inner-coefficient reading the number grows as
+  (\Theta(d)) in the explicit family;
+- even the conjectured (\gamma)-degree bound would leave
+  (\Theta(d)) scalar coefficients per bounded collection of exceptional
+  digits;
+- the two Moh shapes are empty after the normalized Jacobian equation, so
+  they do not establish a rigidity theorem on a nonempty solution locus.
+
+Therefore theorem (T) cannot currently be stated as one finite computation
+independent of (d).  For the intended (q\ge2) descended rows the honest
+status is `OPEN`, not refuted and not proved.
+
+### 6.2 Radii constrain the support of (h); they do not determine (h)
+
+**SOURCE-READ.**  Moh p.208 leaves
+(b_1,\ldots,b_4) free in the ((16,12)) approximate root.  P.211 leaves
+(a_1,\ldots,a_8) free in the ((15,10)) approximate root.  Thus the radii
+and root split select a Newton/support shape, not numerical coefficients of
+(h).  The charged Opus statement that (h) is “determined” by
+((n',m',M_2',V_2')) is too strong.
+
+In general a monic bivariate polynomial of total and (\pi)-degree (d)
+has (\Theta(d^2)) possible lower scalar coefficients before order
+conditions.  The charged count (d(d+1)/2-1) uses additional normalization;
+it is still quadratic.  A proof that radii uniquely determine those values,
+or reduce them to bounded data, is absent.
+
+### 6.3 Denominator shedding repairs the alleged anchor zero
+
+**SOURCE-READ, Moh pp.173--174.**  Prop.5.1 separates two cases:
+
+\[
+M_h=n-1\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
+\delta_h=\delta_{h-1}=-\frac1{n-M_{h-1}-1},
+\]
+
+whereas the denominator (n-M_h-1) is used only when (M_h<n-1).
+The Definition--Remark immediately says that a terminal (M_h=n-1) is
+dropped and that (M_s) means the last *effective* characteristic pair.
+
+The charged `OPEN[DESCENT-ANCHOR]` substituted a raw descended
+(M'=n'-1) into Definition 5.1(3) as if it were effective.  That violates
+the source's denominator-shedding instruction.  Its reported affected sets
+(38/57) at (n\le100), (12/20) at (n=108), and the claimed printed-row
+subset are lane measurements, not source statements; they are retained only
+as the bounded re-audit queue.  The safe disposition is:
+
+```text
+CORRECTED-HERE: zero denominator is artificial; shed terminal M'=n'-1.
+OPEN[DESCENT-RADIUS-REINDEX]: recompute the affected rows on the shortened
+effective tower before claiming coverage or numerical radii.
+```
+
+This is precisely a denominator-shedding issue; no flag, physical place, or
+cover series is identified with another.
+
+### 6.4 The genuine (u_s>1) residual
+
+**SOURCE-READ.**  Prop.6.3 requires the minor-disc bound
+(\delta^*_{s-1}\ge v_s/u_s).  Prop.6.4 supplies it automatically only when
+(u_s=1).  Thus (u_s>1) rows still require either proof of that bound or
+Moh's minor-disc branching.  The charged frequencies (1/58) at
+(n\le100) and (1/20) at (n=108) are `MEASURED / UNREVIEWED`, not a
+source theorem and not permission to omit the rows.
+
+## 7. Residual OPENs, bounded quantities, and cheapest tests
+
+1. **`OPEN[T-LADDER-q>=2]`.**  Bounded quantity: for the two intended
+   reduced pairs ((3,2)) and ((4,3)), the maximum (\gamma)-degree and
+   number of nonconstant inner (\pi)-coefficient functions after canonical
+   carries.  Cheapest test: derive (H)--(C) symbolically for generic
+   (d=1,2,3) without Moh's empty radius slices, branch on vanished leaders,
+   and either find a (q=2) family or isolate the first centralizer/carry
+   lemma that fails.  Do not infer it from the (q=1) family.
+
+2. **`OPEN[MOH-22-ANSATZ]`.**  Bounded quantity: the ten pre-simplification
+   scalar slots missing between the stated main count 22 and the displayed
+   count 12 (and the corresponding (5/6) slots in the bracketed branches).
+   Cheapest test: reconstruct the p.208 order inequalities before applying
+   the p.209 (\eta)-target changes, then verify 22, 15, and 13 by an explicit
+   monomial-basis count.  The pp.210--211 shape alone cannot do this.
+
+3. **`OPEN[FINITE-COMPUTATION-PI-PROFILE]`.**  Bounded quantity: one theorem
+   controlling the (\pi)-support of each exceptional digit, plus the
+   approximate-root moduli.  Cheapest test: enumerate the allowed support
+   polytopes for Moh's (d=4,5) controls and one (d=7) row, and check
+   whether their scalar counts stabilize or grow.  A bound only on
+   (\deg_\gamma) is insufficient.
+
+4. **`OPEN[DESCENT-RADIUS-REINDEX]`.**  Bounded quantity: the reported 38 of
+   57 rows at (n\le100), 12 of 20 at (n=108), and any printed candidates
+   with raw terminal (M'=n'-1).  Cheapest test: delete that terminal pair,
+   recompute (d_i,V_i,s), and rerun the scaled radius formula on the
+   effective tower.  Until then, neither “undefined” nor “fully covered” is
+   licensed.
+
+5. **`OPEN[MINOR-DICHOTOMY]`.**  Bounded quantity: the measured one
+   (u_s>1) survivor in each of the two cited screens.  Cheapest test: for
+   each, compute the actual minor radius and compare it with (v_s/u_s); if
+   the bound fails, enumerate Moh's two minor-disc branches rather than
+   applying Prop.6.4.
+
+## 8. Reproduction and resource bound
+
+Drivers are in `box/tladder-drivers-20260903/`:
