+## 10. Final typed answer
+
+The (\eta)-adic first rung is real and exact, but it is the only proved
+degree-independent rung.  The general (h)-adic equations are the coupled
+bracket-and-carry system (H)--(L); they do not force individual digits to be
+constant without additional centralizer, no-carry, and separation input.
+
+On Moh's shapes, the nonconstant digit degrees happen to satisfy the proposed
+bound before the final Jacobian solve, but both normalized (C\ne0) loci are
+empty.  The ((16,12)) scalar count (17\to10) is reproduced; the requested
+((15,10)) count 22 is from an earlier ansatz than the supplied 12-parameter
+shape and is therefore not reconstructible from it.
+
+Globally, the family in §5 has fixed (k,p,q=1,r), arbitrarily large
+(d=re), and one canonical nonconstant digit of
+(\gamma)-degree (d/r), containing (\Theta(d)) nonconstant inner
+coefficients.  Hence no uniform obstruction, and no finite computation
+independent of (d), follows from T-LADDER as stated.  The restriction
+(q\ge2), together with a separate (\pi)-profile theorem and genuine
+control of the coefficients of (h), remains `OPEN`.

