    96	### 1.2 Ring and variable map
    97	
    98	Throughout the algebraic sections,
    99	
   100	\[
   101	R=k[\gamma,\pi],\qquad
   102	J(F,G)=F_\gamma G_\pi-F_\pi G_\gamma,
   103	\]
   104	
   105	where \(k\) has characteristic zero.  In the drivers the declared map is
   106	\(\gamma\mapsto x,\ \pi\mapsto y\), and coefficient comparison occurs in
   107	\(k[\text{parameters}][x,y]\).  Moh's degree order is
   108	\(n=\deg g>m=\deg f\); thus the task's high-degree \(P\) is Moh's \(g\), and
   109	the lower-degree \(Q\) is Moh's \(f\).
   110	
   111	Moh's Appendix II also names the remainder in
   112	\(\beta^2=\alpha h+\gamma\) by the letter \(\gamma\).  That is not the
   113	Prop.6.3 coordinate.  It is denoted \(\Gamma\) below.  Primes on
   114	\(n',M_i',\delta_i'\) are labels, not derivatives.
   115	
   116	### 1.3 Leading form: what it gives and what it does not
   117	
   118	**PROVED-HERE / UNREVIEWED.**  Suppose
   119	
   120	\[
   121	\deg P=\deg_\pi P=n',\quad
   122	\deg Q=\deg_\pi Q=m',\quad
   123	J(P,Q)=c\gamma^k,\quad k<n'+m'-2.
   124	\]
   125	
   126	The degree-\(n'+m'-2\) part of the Jacobian is
   127	\(J(L_P,L_Q)\), so it vanishes.  For homogeneous binary forms in
   128	characteristic zero, Euler's identity and one-variable dehomogenization give
   129	\(L_P^{m'}=\lambda L_Q^{n'}\).  Unique factorization then gives, after monic
   130	normalization,
   131	
   132	\[
   133	L_P=H_0^p,\qquad L_Q=H_0^q,\qquad
   134	d=\gcd(n',m'),\quad p=n'/d,\quad q=m'/d.
   135	\]
   136	
   137	This identifies a common *leading form* \(H_0\).  It does not determine a
   138	full polynomial approximate root \(h\).  Once a monic \(h\) of
   139	\(\pi\)-degree \(d\) is fixed, Euclidean division gives unique expansions
   140	
   141	\[
   142	P=\sum_{i=0}^{p}A_i h^i,qquad
   143	Q=\sum_{j=0}^{q}B_j h^j,qquad
   144	\deg_\pi A_i,\deg_\pi B_j<d.
   145	\]
   146	
   147	Without the remainder bounds the expansion is nonunique: a multiple of
   148	\(T-h\) may be moved between adjacent digits.  Choosing \(h\) as a normalized
   149	approximate root can kill one penultimate digit; it does not automatically
   150	kill both.  Indeed replacing \(h\) by
   151	\(h+A_{p-1}/p\) kills \(A_{p-1}\), while the other penultimate digit becomes
   152	\(B_{q-1}-(q/p)A_{p-1}\).
   153	
   154	## 2. The exact series rung
   155	
   156	### 2.1 What Moh proves
   157	
   158	**SOURCE-READ.**  Moh's Lemma 2.1, printed p.151, assumes a *constant*
   159	Jacobian.  With
   160	
   161	\[
   162	g=\eta^{-n},\qquad
   163	f=\eta^{-m}+\sum_{j>-m}f_j(x)\eta^j,
   164	\]
   165	
   166	it says that \(J(f,g)\in k^\times\) exactly when the derivatives of the
   167	digits below \(n-1\) vanish and \(f'_{n-1}\) is a nonzero constant.  The
   168	source does not state the monomial-Jacobian version and does not state an
   169	\(h\)-adic version.
   170	
   171	### 2.2 Monomial extension
   172	
   173	**PROVED-HERE / UNREVIEWED.**  Let \(f,g\in k[\gamma,\pi]\) be monic in
   174	\(\pi\), of \(\pi\)-degrees \(m,n\), put
   175	\(\eta=g^{-1/n}=\pi^{-1}+O(\pi^{-2})\), and expand
   176	
   177	\[
   178	f=\eta^{-m}+\sum_{j>-m} f_j(\gamma)\eta^j.
   179	\]
   180	
   181	Then, for \(c\ne0\),
   182	
   183	\[
   184	J(f,g)=c\gamma^k
   185	\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
   186	\begin{cases}
   187	f_j\in k,&j<n-1,\\[2mm]
   188	f_{n-1}=\dfrac{c}{n(k+1)}\gamma^{k+1}+a,&a\in k.
   189	\end{cases}
   190	\]
   191	
   192	At fixed \(\eta\), the chain rule gives
   193	\(J(f,g)=f_\gamma|_\eta\,g_\pi\), and \(g_\pi\) has
   194	\(\eta\)-order \(-(n-1)\) and leading coefficient \(n\).  A nonzero
   195	polynomial of \(\pi\)-degree \(D\) has \(\eta\)-order \(-D\).  This proves
   196	the equivalence and its exact leading scalar.
   197	
   198	In the dual convention \(\xi=f^{-1/m}\),
   199	
   200	\[
   201	g=\xi^{-n}+\sum g_j(\gamma)\xi^j,
   202	\]
   203	
   204	one obtains
   205	
   206	\[
   207	g_j\in k\ (j<m-1),\qquad
   208	g_{m-1}=-\frac{c}{m(k+1)}\gamma^{k+1}+a.
   209	\]
   210	
   211	Thus the charged “degree \(k+1\)” iff is too weak: degree alone permits lower
   212	\(\gamma\)-terms and the wrong leading scalar.  Moh p.210 is a useful
   213	source control for \(k=2\): its first nonconstant displayed series digit is
   214	at index \(m-1=9\) and contains \(x^3\).  It is not a proof that canonical
   215	\(h\)-digits obey the same rule.
   216	
   217	## 3. General \(h\)-adic Jacobian equations
   218	
   219	### 3.1 Formal identity
   220	
   221	**PROVED-HERE / UNREVIEWED.**  Put
   222	
   223	\[
   224	F(T)=\sum_i A_iT^i,\qquad G(T)=\sum_jB_jT^j,\qquad
   225	\delta=[h,-].
   226	\]
   227	
   228	The typed evaluation map is
   229	
   230	\[
   231	\operatorname{ev}_h:R[T]\longrightarrow R,qquad T\longmapsto h,
   232	\]
   233	
   234	with \(T\) treated as independent while differentiating coefficients.  Then
   235	
   236	\[
   237	[P,Q]=\operatorname{ev}_h\bigl([F,G]_R+F_T\delta G-G_T\delta F\bigr).
   238	\]
   239	
   240	Equivalently,
   241	
   242	\[
   243	[P,Q]=\sum_{\ell\ge0}C_\ell h^\ell,
   244	\]
   245	
   246	where, with out-of-range digits set to zero,
   247	
   248	\[
   249	C_\ell=
   250	\sum_{i+j=\ell}[A_i,B_j]
   251	+\sum_{i+j=\ell+1}
   252	\left(iA_i[h,B_j]-jB_j[h,A_i]\right).
   253	\tag{H}
   254	\]
   255	
   256	For example,
   257	
   258	\[
   259	C_0=[A_0,B_0]+A_1[h,B_0]-B_1[h,A_0].
   260	\]
   261	
   262	These are *raw* coefficients in an independent symbol before \(T=h\).
   263	Actual \(h\)-adic comparison needs carries.
   264	
   265	### 3.2 Canonical carry recursion
   266	
   267	Set \(K_0=0\).  Recursively perform monic Euclidean division in
   268	\(k[\gamma][\pi]\):
   269	
   270	\[
   271	C_\ell+K_\ell=R_\ell+hK_{\ell+1},qquad
   272	\deg_\pi R_\ell<d.
   273	\tag{C}
   274	\]
   275	
   276	Continue through every terminal carry.  Then
   277	
   278	\[
   279	[P,Q]=\sum_{\ell\ge0}R_\ell h^\ell,
   280	\]
   281	
   282	and the exact level-by-level condition is
   283	
   284	\[
   285	J(P,Q)=c\gamma^k
   286	\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
   287	R_0=c\gamma^k,qquad R_\ell=0\ (\ell\ge1).
   288	\tag{L}
   289	\]
   290	
   291	It is generally invalid to impose \(C_0=c\gamma^k\) and \(C_\ell=0\)
   292	coefficientwise: products and brackets of digits can have
   293	\(\pi\)-degree at least \(d\) and feed higher levels via (C).  Formally, the
   294	Jacobian condition says that the independent-\(T\) expression differs from
   295	\(c\gamma^k\) by a multiple of \(T-h\); it does not say its \(T\)-coefficients
   296	vanish.
   297	
   298	Even in an added no-carry model, the top-down equations usually control one
   299	combination per rung.  Writing \(a_r=A_{p-r}\), \(b_r=B_{q-r}\), with
   300	\(a_0=b_0=1\), the raw level \(p+q-r-1\) has the form
   301	
   302	\[
   303	C_{p+q-r-1}=
   304	\sum_{a+b=r+1}[a_a,b_b]
   305	+\sum_{a+b=r}
   306	\bigl((p-a)a_a\delta b_b-(q-b)b_b\delta a_a\bigr).
   307	\]
   308	
   309	If \(a_1=0\) and \(b_1\in k\), it reduces to
   310	\(\delta(pb_r-qa_r)+\Psi_r\), with \(\Psi_r\) involving earlier levels.
   311	It does not separately make \(a_r\) and \(b_r\) constant.  A further
   312	centralizer hypothesis such as
   313	\(\ker[h,-]=k[h]\) would turn a lower-\(\pi\)-degree element in the kernel
   314	into a constant, but that hypothesis is neither automatic nor sufficient to
   315	separate the two digits.
   316	
   317	### 3.3 Correction of the charged \((2,3)\) display
   318	
   319	For
   320	
   321	\[
   322	f=h^2+2\beta,\qquad g=h^3+G_1h+G_0,
   323	\]
   324	
   325	direct Leibniz expansion gives
   326	
   327	\[
   328	\boxed{
   329	\begin{aligned}
   330	J(f,g)={}&h^2\bigl(2J(h,G_1)+6J(\beta,h)\bigr)\\
   331	&+h\bigl(2J(h,G_0)+2J(\beta,G_1)\bigr)\\
   332	&+2G_1J(\beta,h)+2J(\beta,G_0).
   333	\end{aligned}}
   334	\tag{2,3}
   335	\]
   336	
   337	The charged formula shifts \(6J(\beta,h)\) from the \(h^2\)-coefficient to
   338	the \(h\)-coefficient and omits \(2J(\beta,G_1)\).  Actual minus charged is
   339	
   340	\[
   341	6(h^2-h)J(\beta,h)+2hJ(\beta,G_1).
   342	\]
   343	
   344	The exact control
   345	
   346	\[
   347	h=\pi,\quad\beta=\gamma/2,\quad G_1=3\gamma/2,\quad G_0=0
   348	\]
   349	
   350	has \(J(f,g)=3\gamma/2\), whereas the charged right side is
   351	\(-3\pi^2+3\pi+3\gamma/2\).
   352	
   353	In Moh's specialization \(G_1=3\beta,\ G_0=3\alpha/2\), the \(h^2\)-terms
   354	cancel.  If \(\beta^2=\alpha h+\Gamma\), (2,3) simplifies further to
   355	
   356	\[
   357	J(f,g)=3\bigl(J(\beta,\alpha)-J(h,\Gamma)\bigr).
   358	\]
   359	
   360	Even this expression must be interpreted through exact polynomial identities,
   361	not by treating unreduced powers of \(h\) as independent.
   362	
